{"ts":"2026-08-16T01:18:44.626Z","author":"pi-agent","author_source":"asserted","agent_harness":"pi","agent_model":"glm-5.3","agent_model_source":"environment","agent_instance":"8dcb74ce2c0330177a04d090","agent_provenance":{"model":{"value":"glm-5.3","source":"environment"},"effort":null,"role":null,"topic":null},"op":"comment_add","patch":[{"op":"replace","path":"/metadata/updated_at","value":"2026-08-16T01:18:44.626Z"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/comments","value":[{"created_at":"2026-08-16T01:18:44.626Z","author":"pi-agent","text":"Regression slice on branch release-pipeline-regression-contract-tests. New test dangling_identity_test_cannot_pass_vacuously in tests/identity_audit.rs builds the dangling scenario via a shared builder and asserts BOTH: (a) the bad identity (bad@unreachable.example) appears in NO reachable author (git log --all --format=%ae must be non-empty and clean — guarding against the empty-repo vacuity), and (b) the audit still rejects the repository, which with a clean ref walk can only happen via the unreachable commit. Release-workflow half: release_commit_identity_is_allowlisted_and_justified extracts every 'git config user.email' from the release job and requires each to be allowlisted, plus a justification comment referencing release.yml. Revert-proofs (both historical defects reproduced): mutating the audit to enumerate commits with 'git rev-list --all' instead of cat-file --batch-all-objects made the vacuity test FAIL (plus both dangling-object tests); removing --reset-author from the amend — the original vacuous form — made it FAIL with 'the bad identity is reachable from refs, so the dangling-object test proves nothing'; deleting the 41898282+github-actions[bot] identity from approved-git-identities.txt made the allowlist test FAIL. All restored, all green."}]}],"before_hash":"8dbd08f4754cda073d01795c4eed2fd35248fb83cbcd8e540b7094c111489136","after_hash":"4b0d5f730fee747b5fba71c2291d0c934578110b8d98b3804ba9a748fcebb3a9","item_hash_version":2}
